 \ReviewerComment{L. 110 – a reader does not know to this point what reference is. I am also not sure weather persistence and climatology are reliable references…. what if a predictor like lsp would be a reference? Consider it together with major comment 2.}
 
-\PendingResponse
+\AuthorResponse{We thank the reviewer for these points.
 
-\ChangeLocation
+\textit{Meaning of ``reference''.} We agree that the term was used before it was defined. At its first occurrences, in the Abstract and in the first study objective (formerly L.~110, now in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:gaps_objectives}), it now reads ``statistical reference forecasts (climatology, persistence and linear regression)'', and the objective points to their definitions in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:benchmark_models}.
+
+\textit{Persistence and climatology.} We agree that they are not sufficient references on their own. They are minimum-skill references: a useful forecast should outperform both, but doing so does not establish practical skill. For this reason the revised manuscript also includes training-fitted UTC-hour, seasonal, monthly and month-by-hour climatologies, an AR(1) damped persistence, Ridge regressions on all 42 inputs (L1) and on the seven origin-time inputs (L2), and OLS on origin-time tp and lsp (L3), all reported in Table~\ref{manuscript-tab:strong_baselines}. Ridge L1 is the strongest of them (RMSE 0.249733~mm\,h$^{-1}$), and only XGBoost, BiLSTM-Attention and BU-LSTM-Attention had statistically supported RMSE reductions relative to it. We added a sentence to Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:benchmark_models} stating the role of persistence and climatology.
+
+\textit{lsp as a reference.} OLS L3 is the reference closest to the reviewer's suggestion: a least-squares regression of the six-hour-ahead target on precipitation and lsp at the forecast origin, fitted on training data only. It had essentially the same test RMSE as AR(1), which uses precipitation alone (0.262894 and 0.262659~mm\,h$^{-1}$), so origin-time lsp did not improve on precipitation persistence in this linear reference. Relative to L3, RMSE was lower for all six learning models, by 0.0038 to 0.0200~mm\,h$^{-1}$, with paired circular-block intervals excluding zero for all but the Transformer, and ROC AUC at Q95 and 1.0~mm\,h$^{-1}$ was higher for all six models. These paired results were previously archived but not reported; they are now in Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:stronger_baseline_results}. We did not construct a reference that uses lsp alone as the forecast value.
+
+As discussed in our response to Major Comment~2, a reference built from lsp must use lsp at or before the forecast origin. The target-hour lsp is not available when the forecast is issued, and the lsp of the latest input hour does not bound the target: among 430 training forecast origins with latest lsp of at least 0.7~mm, the target was below that value in 87.9\% of cases. The ablation results reported there likewise show that lsp, mxtpr and mntpr carry overlapping and modest predictive information.}
